      "math_academy_id": "q-16096",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $-2$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $10$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=-4.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=-4} & =f(-4){g}^{′}(-4)+g(-4){f}^{′}(-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 10+(-7)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
